{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01A5idXPSfxoV8LxCBLTWsi4","type":"tool_result","content":"196\t    // Source order is `0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf(...))`, but GCC commutes the\n197\t    // outer add and emits `ADDSS dst=sqrtf(...), src=(dy2 + dx2)` (see\n198\t    // `tfm+0x65` / `tfm+0x103` in the reference object). That choice is\n199\t    // observable: when both the square root and the `dy2 + dx2` sum are NaN --\n200\t    // e.g. `dxy` is a NaN while `dy2 + dx2` is `inf + -inf`, which yields the\n201\t    // indefinite `0xFFC00000` -- the destination operand's payload is the one\n202\t    // that survives. So the square root has to be the destination here.\n203\t    //\n204\t    // The inner `dy2 + dx2` keeps the source order. GCC hoists it above the\n205\t    // branch as `ADDSS dst=src[0], src=src[1]`, which is already exactly this\n206\t    // order in the `else` branch (`dy2 == src[0]`); in the `if` branch the\n207\t    // operands are swapped relative to the source, but there `src[0] < src[1]`\n208\t    // held, so neither operand can be NaN and the difference is unobservable.\n209\t    //\n210\t    // The `* 0.5f` is likewise emitted as `MULSS dst=sum, src=0.5f`, but only\n211\t    // one of the two operands can ever be NaN, so either order agrees.\n212\t    fmul(0.5f32, fadd(fsqrt(clamp_nonneg_c(sqd)), fadd(dy2, dx2)))\n213\t}\n214\t\n215\t/// `void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count);`\n216\t///\n217\t/// Consumes `count` triples of floats from `src` and writes `count` pairs of\n218\t/// floats to `dest`.\n219\t///\n220\t/// # Safety\n221\t///\n222\t/// Same contract as the C function: `src` must be valid for reading\n223\t/// `3 * count` floats and `dest` must be valid for writing `2 * count` floats\n224\t/// whenever `count > 0`. Non-positive `count` performs no accesses at all,\n225\t/// exactly as the C `for` loop does.\n226\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n227\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn tfm(dest: *mut f32, src: *const f32, count: c_int) {\n228\t    // Local mutable copies of the pointer parameters; C advances the\n229\t    // parameters themselves at the bottom of each iteration.\n230\t    let mut dest: *mut f32 = dest;\n231\t    let mut src: *const f32 = src;\n232\t\n233\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n234\t    while i < count {\n235\t        let s0: f32 = unsafe { *src.add(0) };\n236\t        let s1: f32 = unsafe { *src.add(1) };\n237\t        let s2: f32 = unsafe { *src.add(2) };\n238\t\n239\t        // NaN in either operand makes this false, selecting the `else` branch,\n240\t        // exactly as C's `<` does."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39ed02e0-9676-4095-9dea-9966bf04ae5f","uuid":"df0d033e-340b-4505-9833-f1499ef4a585","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:21:43.086Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-msBkuo/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"    // Source order is `0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf(...))`, but GCC commutes the\n    // outer add and emits `ADDSS dst=sqrtf(...), src=(dy2 + dx2)` (see\n    // `tfm+0x65` / `tfm+0x103` in the reference object). That choice is\n    // observable: when both the square root and the `dy2 + dx2` sum are NaN --\n    // e.g. `dxy` is a NaN while `dy2 + dx2` is `inf + -inf`, which yields the\n    // indefinite `0xFFC00000` -- the destination operand's payload is the one\n    // that survives. So the square root has to be the destination here.\n    //\n    // The inner `dy2 + dx2` keeps the source order. GCC hoists it above the\n    // branch as `ADDSS dst=src[0], src=src[1]`, which is already exactly this\n    // order in the `else` branch (`dy2 == src[0]`); in the `if` branch the\n    // operands are swapped relative to the source, but there `src[0] < src[1]`\n    // held, so neither operand can be NaN and the difference is unobservable.\n    //\n    // The `* 0.5f` is likewise emitted as `MULSS dst=sum, src=0.5f`, but only\n    // one of the two operands can eve