{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016b86hUKTwGVMXgMr4kGREn","type":"tool_result","content":"   Compiling translation v0.1.0 (/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-msBkuo/translation)\n    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.14s\n=== exported symbol ===\n0000000000011cb0 T tfm\n=== crate-type ===\n\nDynamic section at offset 0x4c278 contains 28 entries:\n  Tag        Type                         Name/Value","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39ed02e0-9676-4095-9dea-9966bf04ae5f","uuid":"e96bc632-7d44-4a58-a7d0-f285f8a09390","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:23:24.572Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"   Compiling translation v0.1.0 (/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-msBkuo/translation)\n    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.14s\n=== exported symbol ===\n0000000000011cb0 T tfm\n=== crate-type ===\n\nDynamic section at offset 0x4c278 contains 28 entries:\n  Tag        Type                         Name/Value","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_4qzbafmr2vzrfolwspnfg7hh2pbuoe7j27conb7nx5jqa4wmw23a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Clean release build, exporting exactly the C library's public ABI.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` is small: `CMakeLists.txt`, a one-line header, and a 32-line `lib.c`. `nm -D` on the C `.so` and the public header agree — the entire public surface is **one symbol, `tfm`**, with no namespace/renaming macros. `tfm` walks `count` triples `(dx2, dy2, dxy)` and writes `count` pairs, computing a structure-tensor-style eigen decomposition.\n\n**Symbol gate: `comm -23` of C vs Rust exports is empty, and there are no extra exports. 1-for-1.**\n\n## The interesting part\n\nA literal transliteration compiled fine and produced the same arithmetic — but a differential fuzz harness (dlopen both `.so`s, `memcmp` raw bits) found **406 mismatches out of 3.7M**, all involving NaN/inf.\n\nReading both disassemblies showed why: FP add/mul are commutative for ordinary values, so **LLVM freely swaps operands and auto-vectorizes** (`mulps`, `addss %xmm7,%xmm6`). They are *not* commutative in NaN payload propagation — x86 returns the quieted **destination** operand if it is NaN, and only otherwise the source. So `(-nan) + (+nan)` yields `-nan` or `+nan` purely based on register assignment. Inputs like `(0.0, -inf, NaN)` reach this, because `2.0f*dx2*dy2` becomes `-inf*0.0` → the x86 \"QNaN indefinite\" `0xFFC00000`, which then races against the input NaN.\n\nFix: compute each op normally (bit-exact whenever the result isn't NaN) and, only on the rare NaN outcome, impose x86's deterministic selection rules in a `#[cold]` helper. Since every NaN outcome is now decided explicitly, the optimizer may still reorder freely, and GCC's actual chosen operand order is reproduced at each call site. That took 406 → 4.\n\nThe last 4 came from GCC **itself** commuting an add. I then found something worth flagging:\n\n> **The C library is not self-consistent.** `-O0`, `-O1`/`-O2`/`-Os`, and `-O3` builds of `c_src` disagree with *each other* on these NaN payloads, because GCC picks different operand orders per level. No single implementation can match all three.\n\nThis is resolvable, not a coin flip. The divergence requires a NaN *input*: with finite inputs every NaN that can arise is the canonical `0xFFC00000`, so payload selection can't matter. I verified all builds agree on finite inputs. For NaN inputs I matched the build `c_src`'s own `CMakeLists.txt` produces — `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` is unset, so no `-O` flag, i.e. `-O0`, which is also what a plain `gcc -shared` glob gives. Two details confirmed empirically rather than assumed: the `.rodata` constants (`4.0f`, `0.0f`, `0.5f`), and that glibc's `sqrtf@plt` (which `-O0` really calls) returns exactly `quiet(x)` for NaN.\n\nAlso preserved deliberately: the two C branches bind `dx2`/`dy2` to *opposite* inputs, so despite textually identical formulas they are genuinely different rounding sequences; and `(((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))` is not `fmaxf` — it returns NaN for NaN and `-0.0f` for `-0.0f`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **24M** NaN/inf-heavy elements vs the reference build, 8 seeds — 0 mismatches\n- **54M** finite elements vs *every* opt level (`-O0/1/2/3/s` + cmake) — 0 mismatches\n- Special-value grid (all 35,937 triples of ±0, denormals, `FLT_MAX`, ±inf, ±NaN), branch-boundary probing at 1-ULP spacing, signaling-NaN payloads, batches to n=100,000, and `count <= 0` (confirmed neither library writes)\n\nI removed the reference `.so`s afterward so nothing shadows the real artifact; `c_src/` is byte-for-byte untouched.\n\nOne caveat to state plainly: for **NaN inputs**, byte-identity is only well-defined relative to a chosen C build. I targeted the `-O0`/CMake default. If your harness builds `c_src` with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` (`-O3`) *and* feeds NaNs, those cases would need the `-O3` operand order instead — that's a two-line change in `lambda_of`, and I can switch it or put it behind a feature flag if you know which build the harness uses."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":536,"cache_read_input_tokens":89664,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":536,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39ed02e0-9676-4095-9dea-9966bf04ae5f","uuid":"eaf1c35a-5410-4314-9992-662db464d91a","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:23:48.204Z"}
